# BUG WITNESS, not a benchmark: the SILENT half of the mangling bug. Our Java
# backend prints 13 where the program says 42, with no exception and no build
# failure.
#
# Arend-authored (ExampleAxiomCollision.axiomCollisionProgram): prog.ast is
# `ExamplePrint:axiomCollisionSexpr` and prog.attr is
# `ExamplePrint:axiomCollisionAttrsOCamlText`, both regenerated by
# test/tools/regen-arend-asts.sh.
#
# The whole program is one axiom applied to two primitive-int literals:
#
#   axiom (mpFile ["_Nat"]) "add" : int -> int -> int      -- no body
#   main = _Nat.add 6 7
#
# and prog.attr remaps that axiom onto int63 MULTIPLICATION
# (`Prim_int63.mul`, from runtime/int63/prim_int63.ml). So the program's own
# declared meaning is 6 * 7 = 42, which is what the ocaml backend prints.
#
# The mechanism. `compileDecl` (ToJava.ard:355) resolves a bodiless constant
# with `lookupAxiom name javaAxioms` where `name` is the ALREADY-MANGLED
# kername, and the table's keys (ToJava.ard:133-183) are mangled strings.
# Because `joinDirs` separates DirPath components with `_` and leaves `_` inside
# a component unescaped (see ../mangle-collision/meta), the intended-unrelated
# axiom collides with the genuine Lean `Nat.add`:
#
#   mangleKername (mpDot (mpFile nil) "Nat", "add")  ==  "c__Nat__add"   <- the key
#   mangleKername (mpFile ["_Nat"],          "add")  ==  "c__Nat__add"   <- this program
#
# so `lookupAxiom` hits the `Nat.add` entry and the generated method returns
# `Rt.PRIM_ADD_LONG`, printing 13. The comment at ToJava.ard:129-131 states the
# opposite intent -- "Keeping the module path avoids treating an unrelated
# `Foo.add` as Nat.add" -- and the module path IS kept; it is just not kept
# injectively.
#
# What SHOULD happen is `compileDecl`'s `nothing` branch: a method that throws
# "axiom c__Nat__add not implemented". A loud failure on an axiom our runtime
# cannot realize is the designed behaviour, and it is what an injective mangling
# would restore. The fix is the same one ../mangle-collision/ needs -- and note
# it must land in `joinDirs` AND in `javaAxioms`'s keys together, since those
# keys are mangled text.
#
# 6, 7 and 42 are tiny on purpose: unlike ../../handwritten/matmul/, this has nothing to do
# with the int63-vs-64-bit-long mismatch. Both backends agree on the arithmetic;
# they disagree on WHICH operation the program named.
#
# `c` is excluded: `peregrine c` refuses any program with unrealized axioms
# ("Axioms found, use Extract Constant to realize them in C"), see
# ../../../runtime/peano/README.md. Realizing it in C would not change what the
# Java backend does, which is the finding here.
#
# `xfail=` is deliberately EMPTY: this is a real, open bug, so the row is a
# genuine `wrong(java)` and `run.py --all` exits non-zero until it is fixed.
#
# The row reads `wrong(java)` and NOT `wrong(java) disagree`, even though the two
# backends do disagree (13 vs 42): `result_of` (run.py:226-245) returns the
# failure list as soon as it is non-empty and only falls through to the
# `disagree` check when every backend passed. That is deliberate there -- the
# `disagree` oracle exists for programs with no `expected` value -- so the
# disagreement is visible in the two work/*/output.txt files rather than in the
# status word. Not worth changing run.py for.
#
# Two notes for whoever writes the next Arend-authored program, both learned
# here the hard way:
#
# * The entry point is the CONSTANT `res`, not the application `_Nat.add 6 7`.
#   Malfunction module fields are POSITIONAL and the runtime bundles' `.mli`
#   files declare a single `val main`, so the OCaml driver reads export SLOT 0 --
#   which is the constant `main` refers to, not `$main`. With an application as
#   `main`, slot 0 held the remapped `Prim_int63.mul` CLOSURE and the driver
#   printed it as an int (47978189223556). Every other program in the corpus
#   happens to satisfy the invariant because its `main` is a bare `tConst`
#   (`matmul` exports `($sumRes $main)`, both 2197000), so nothing caught it
#   before.
#
# * This program reuses the `int63` bundle, whose OCAML_MODULE is `matmul` and
#   whose OCAML_EXTRA already ships `prim_int63.ml` -- which is why the axiom can
#   be remapped onto `Prim_int63.mul` with no new runtime bundle.
expected=42
backends=java ocaml
runtime=int63
xfail=
note=BUG: javaAxioms is keyed by MANGLED names, so axiom _Nat.add collides with Nat.add and is silently realized as PRIM_ADD_LONG (13) instead of the declared multiplication (42)
